  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/b0/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_3.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "After Spider-Man stops three small-time crooks on page 2, it turns out to be so easy that he actually begins to wish, \"for an opponent who'd give me a run for my money!\"\n\nAt that very moment, at the \"Atomic Research Center,\" we're introduced to Otto Octavius, better known as \"Doctor Octopus\" because of the mechanism with the four arms that he controls to perform nuclear experiments, while he himself is protected behind a lead shield.\n\nNaturally, there's a malfunction, and Octavius is caught in an explosion and absorbs a great deal of radiation, causing some brain damage and the fusing of his arm mechanism to his body.&nbsp; He awakens and wants to return to his work, but they say he needs rest.&nbsp; His damaged brain reasons that they must be jealous of him.&nbsp; His arms now obey his commands and possess great strength, and with their help, he soon takes over the hospital.\n\nMeanwhile, JJJ gives Peter Parker the assignment of getting photos of the injured scientist, since none of his own photographers have been allowed in.&nbsp; Spider-Man is soon on the job!&nbsp; Climbing up the hospital wall, he sees Dr. Octopus threatening a member of the staff, and he crashes through the window to face off with the now-deranged scientist.\n\nSpider-Man webs two of his arms together, but Ock has two others to hold his foe with, and he soon snaps the webbing (to Spider-Man's horror).&nbsp; Octavius now holds Spider-Man with all four arms, his strength being greatly increased by the accident.&nbsp; He smacks Spider-Man in the face and then tosses him out the window, saying \"You're no threat to me!\"&nbsp; Outside, Spider-Man picks himself up, completely dejected at his first defeat, his confidence now shattered, as he believes he never even had a <i>chance</i>.&nbsp; Is this to be the end of Spider-Man?\n\nOctavius has to leave the hospital now since all his hostages fled during the brief fight.&nbsp; He returns to the atomic research center, scaling walls with his arms and walking with them as well.&nbsp; He wants to take over the \"greatest source of atomic power in the nation.\"&nbsp; He soon starts making drastic and dangerous changes to the plant, driving out many of the workers.\n\nBack at the home of May Parker, Peter is completely dejected, thinking his career as Spider-Man is over.&nbsp; When he gets to school, he learns that the government has asked the Fantastic Four to try and capture Doctor Octopus.&nbsp; The whole team was busy, but they did send the Human Torch, who apparently is suffering from a virus (which we don't learn until the last page), and while he waits to heal before he goes off to face Dr. Octopus, he is planning to speak to their assembly today.&nbsp; Peter is unimpressed.&nbsp; However, Johnny finishes his talk by challenging them to \"never give up!...Even the Fantastic Four have had defeats -- but we always come back!\"&nbsp; Peter is inspired by the talk and approaches Johnny afterward to thank him.\n\nPeter heads home and suits up again, determined to bring down Octavius.&nbsp; He easily enters the plant, but is spotted by Octavius on a monitor, and he attempts to stop Spider-Man by throwing various obstacles, but his reflexes make it easy to avoid them.&nbsp; But Peter isn't going straight for his foe, stopping instead at one of the chemical labs to whip up something, which he puts in test tubes and then attaches to wires.\n\nDr. Octopus surprises Spider-Man and attacks.&nbsp; Peter is only able to wrap two of his arms together with the wires before the remaining pair knocks the other wire out of his hand.&nbsp; But the damage is done -- the chemicals fuse two of the arms together.&nbsp; But no matter -- Dr. Octopus still has two more!&nbsp; He corners Spider-Man and attempts to wrap him up and club him with the two fused arms, but Spider-Man's agility keeps him a step ahead, even while he is draped by the other two arms.&nbsp; Webbing his opponent's face gives him a chance to finally draw close to Dr. Octopus and give him the one good punch that knocks him out.&nbsp; After all, it's the arms that are super-powered, not the man.&nbsp; He wraps him up securely with his webbing and leaves him for the authorities.\n\nSpider-Man then finds the hotel where the Human Torch is staying.&nbsp; The doctor is now giving him his approval to use his flame again so he can go catch Dr. Octopus when Spider-Man climbs through the window to tell him there is now no need.&nbsp; He tells Johnny that it's all thanks to him.&nbsp; Otherwise, Spider-Man might have been finished too, leaving Johnny terribly confused, wondering what exactly he did.\n\nJohnny returns to the school the next day to give the kids a proper demonstration of his full powers, but Peter no longer cares.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A remarkably strong early Spider‑Man story with real emotional and visual impact.\nA couple of quirks remain, but the overall experience is undeniably excellent.",
  "subtitle": "A Lesson in Failing",
  "review_text": "This marks the first full-length Spider-Man story, and it was quite good.\n\n[ASSET: right | OttoOctavius | width=17%] The introduction and subsequent origin of the new \"Doctor Octopus\" (Otto Octavius) was right on the money, and while it relied heavily (once again) on radiation as a catalyst, as super-villain origins go, it was believable and even compassion-inducing in the sense that, over the course of the next two and a half pages, a brilliant scientist was caught up in a horrific accident that not only fused his mechanical arms to his body but also adversely affected his brain.\n[BR]\n[BR] Unlike a character such as Doctor Doom, Octavius never set out to subjugate the Earth.&nbsp; He was simply a scientist trying to do his job, unfortunately having his life turned upside-down by this accident.\n[BR]\n[BR] It's a reminder that, but for the grace of God, it could have happened to Peter Parker as well.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And <i>yes</i>, I'm not forgetting that it's a comic book in search of a new adversary for the title hero -- I totally <i>get</i> that, but it seemed important to say nonetheless.)]\n\nBecause of the brain damage, Octavius goes from sympathetic figure to all-out super-villain by the end of page 5:\n[ASSET: center | DocOckIsBorn | width=67%]\n\nWhether the rapid shift is one of the issue's strengths or not is immaterial -- the reader barely has time to process sympathy before menace takes over.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | SpiderManDefeated | width=10%] Pages 6-9 were simply excellent, showing the brash (and still inexperienced) Spider-Man arriving at the hospital, crashing into the facility with the confidence that he can tackle anyone, before actually coming face-to-face with someone whose mechanical arms allow him to fight back (and actually snap his webbing), entangle him relatively easily, and without fanfare literally toss him out the window like so much garbage.\n[BR]\n[BR] It was an eye-opening sequence which jars the young hero back to the reality that he <i>isn't </i>invincible and should not be so quick to wish for an opponent worth his mettle.&nbsp; Ditko's presentation reinforces this by drawing Spider-Man with almost gangly energy -- confident in <i>motion</i>, but not yet seasoned in combat.\n\nIn the moment, Spider-Man is totally defeated, and by the top of page 10, we see a <i>completely</i> deflated Peter Parker doubting his very future as Spider-Man.\n[ASSET: center | ConfidenceGone | width=67%]\n\nIt's a stark contrast to the hero we saw only four pages earlier, who declared, \"It's <i>great</i> being Spider-Man!&nbsp; I can do almost <i>anything</i>!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=What a turn of events!]\n\nI do wonder if the now-dejected Peter was perhaps a bit quick to come down on himself after his initial defeat by this brand-new foe, especially one with mechanical arms that gave him an edge he wasn't expecting.&nbsp; <i>Surely</i> he didn't think his entire career was over due to a single defeat!&nbsp; Perhaps this is just part of his persona, and someone else might not have had the same reaction.\n\n(More on this later, but I guess Peter never watched <i>Underdog</i>, who always said \"If at first you fail your deed, try again till you succeed.\")\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe story takes a break from Peter/Spider-Man here to focus on Doctor Octopus over the course of the next couple of pages.&nbsp; Octavius suddenly decides to abandon the hospital because his hostages escaped, and he suddenly is worried about the arrival of the police.&nbsp; This is curious since he <i>literally</i> just defeated Spider-Man with ease and just threw him out the window without a second thought.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=Why then should he suddenly be worried about the police?]\n\nCould it be that Octavius, despite his new power, hasn't yet internalized his transformation?&nbsp; His instincts are still those of <i>scientist</i>, not conqueror.\n\nWith the aid of his arms, he easily slips into the Atomic Research Center where we first saw him, slips past its security guards, and takes over the control hub with the intention of demonstrating to the world that he is not only powerful, but now also in possession of one of the greatest sources of atomic energy on the planet.\n\n[ASSET: left | FantasticPanel | width=18%] And this is as good a time as any to acknowledge the absolutely <i>fantastic</i> upper-right panel on page 12, showing a deranged Dr. Octopus in complete control, being bathed in the light of the control panel.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What a fantastic panel!]\n[BR]\n[BR] <i>Well done</i>, Steve Ditko --  <i>easily</i> a finalist for possible Best Panel of the Year honors!\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe guest appearance by the depowered Human Torch on page 13 was brief, and it isn't explained until the final page that Johnny is suffering from a virus that keeps him from fully using his powers.&nbsp; We might even pause to wonder why it was that the Fantastic Four sent Johnny out to tackle Doctor Octopus on his <i>own</i> (see top of page 13 and again on 21), especially knowing he wasn't up to full strength.\n\nWhile that might be a perfectly legitimate question to ask, let's not overlook that this was [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=never the point of the scene].\n\nJohnny's speech inspired Peter to go out and try again -- not to allow one defeat to define one's entire identity, because <i>everyone</i> fails from time to time.&nbsp; And in that respect, his appearance here is exactly what the doctor ordered -- an example of a guest hero being used to elevate the protagonist's mindset rather than the action.\n\nPeter heads home and immediately sets out to take down his foe.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe final sequence between Spider-Man and Doctor Octopus takes place over the action-filled pages 14-19, and for the second issue in a row, we get to see Peter in the role of science whiz, taking advantage of that skill to find a way to assist in the downfall of his enemy (recall the inverter he whipped up last issue against the Vulture).\n\nIt was great to see him using all the resources at his disposal, with even Octavius being forced to acknowledge (bottom of page 15) that \"he's cleverer than I thought!\"&nbsp; It's a reminder that Spider-Man's greatest asset perhaps <i>isn't</i> his strength -- it's his ability to think under pressure.\n\nWith two of Dr. Octopus' arms fused together, Spider-Man eventually gets close enough to deliver the knockout blow, and the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved.]\n[ASSET: center | FinalDefeat | width=67%]\n\nOnce again, Steve's artwork is perfect for this character (and even for Doctor Octopus, I might add).&nbsp; Both characters are quirky in their movements.&nbsp; Both are also relatively normal in size and with little-to-no musculature, which makes Ditko the perfect artist here.\n\nOn the other hand, Steve's rendition of Johnny Storm and the Human Torch is equally quirky, and since we're not used to seeing him depicted that way, he comes off looking a little goofier and not at all as larger-than-life as Kirby depicts him monthly over in <i>Fantastic Four</i>, but as he <b>is</b> only a guest star, this is entirely forgivable (and thus no reduction in grade is warranted).&nbsp; His appearance in the book is flawlessly pulled off -- I might add.\n\nFinally, I just have to mention how impressive it is that in Doc Ock's very first appearance, he's already reached the status of [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"the world's most dreaded super-villain!\"] (as stated on the cover).&nbsp; <i>Wow!</i>  To Thor, Hulk, Iron Man, Ant-Man and the FF:&nbsp; I'm sure they are all glad good old Spider-Man was up to the task!&nbsp; (See also my review of [LINK:1963:FF_16|FF #16|2026-09-14].)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis is an extremely good piece of work for its day, and even better than the Vulture story from the previous issue.&nbsp; Dr. Octopus made for an intriguing, not to mention deadly foe for Spider-Man and was visually compelling.&nbsp; The guest appearance by the Human Torch was satisfying, not at <i>all</i> overplayed, and contributed directly to boosting Peter's confidence.\n\nAnd having Spider-Man show up at the end to actually <i>thank</i> him on the final page, leaving the Torch to scratch his head, wondering \"I don't <i>get it</i>!&nbsp; What did <i>I</i> do?\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Chef's kiss!]\n\n[ASSET: right | HesRight | width=12%] While one can't help but marvel at the awesome panel on page 12, which is certainly Ditko's best since the one where Spider-Man finally comes face-to-face with the burglar who killed his Uncle Ben, one also shouldn't discount the look of confidence on Peter's face after hearing what Johnny Storm has to say.\n[BR]\n[BR] It may not have been as visually dynamic as the \"evil Ock\" look, but it was nonetheless effective!\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Once again, great job, Steve!]\n\nThis full-length story was an excellent early adventure for Spider-Man where the creators used the supporting characters believably (not overdoing it by including every single one), Peter's own skills, not to mention the guest appearance to bolster a solid plot and intriguing new villain.\n\nFor Spider-Man, this was every bit as good a story as last year's team-up of Doctor Doom and the Sub-Mariner, pitted against the Fantastic Four.\n\nThe final grade is a well-deserved 9.0 while still making allowances for:\n[HANG] 1) the quick way that Peter got down on himself (but there was a narrative reason for that)\n[HANG] 2) Ock's worry about the police (which caused a momentary head-scratch).\n\nEven though these minor flaws need to be acknowledged, neither one derailed the story much at all and only ensured that it wasn't <i>perfect</i>, not that it wasn't [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=excellent].\n\nDoctor Octopus promises to be a wonderful new addition to the Spider-Man universe, so well done on that score, and from a plot standpoint, the clever use of the Torch as a guest star to help steer the title hero in the right direction and restore his confidence was the right (and very understated) move.\n\nFor an early installment, this issue shows <i>remarkable</i> confidence -- as if the creators clearly understood the kind of stories this hero could carry.",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] This issue features a full-page pin-up \"from your pal, Spider-Man.\"\n\n[HANG0] This seems to be the first appearance of the Spider-Signal.&nbsp; This is an excellent addition to his costume, and even though he makes no mention of it during his uniform revamp during the Vulture storyline from last issue, we can assume that he simply continued to tweak his uniform in the time since.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=It's a great visual] and immediately elicits terror in the minds of lesser, street-level criminals.\n[BR]\n[BR] (And we can even forgive Ditko for the fact that it shows up perfectly round with no distortion, even though Spider-Man is projecting it down from an angle.&nbsp; In this case, the effect is so good as to overlook that sort of nitpicking!)\n\n[ASSET: right | SuperMan | width=14%] [HANG0] No review of this issue would be complete without a callout to the obvious blunder made by letterer John Duffy at the bottom of page 8, where he writes in \"<b>Super-Man</b>\" instead of \"Spider-Man.\"&nbsp; We can assume that it was the letterer at fault, because I hardly think that Stan's script would have misidentified the title hero, despite how poor a memory he was known to have had.&nbsp; In the end, the letterer is the one who actually puts pen to paper, so... there <i>you have it</i>.\n[BR]\n[BR] This has been corrected in the various reprints of the book since then but is included in the one-shot <i>Marvel No-Prize</i> collection book, published in the early 80s, complete with the blurb about them being lucky DC's lawyers were apparently asleep that month and missed it, <i>heh</i>.\n\n[HANG0] Continuity Note:&nbsp; this is not the first time Spider-Man and the Human Torch have come face-to-face.&nbsp; That happened in [LINK:1962:ASM_1_asm1|ASM #1] of course, but it's the first time they've spoken without the other members of the FF being present.&nbsp; It seemed only natural for the Marvel creators to want to pair the two teenaged heroes.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: See the upcoming <i>Strange Tales</i> Annual for the next encounter.]"
  ],
  "letters_page": "This is the first <i>Spider's Web</i> letters page, which includes an early letter by Fred Bronson, who seemed to like the new hero.\n\nPhil Leibfred expresses his hope for the future by concluding with:&nbsp; \"Here's to Spider-Man's running 50 years in his own comic.\" with the editorial response being:&nbsp; <i>Only fifty???</i>  (Heh, if they only knew....)\n\nBuddy Saunders loves Steve Ditko's work on ASM, saying \"Mr. Ditko has a knack for drawing a man so that he seems half-man and half-spider - the Spider-Man.&nbsp; His style in SM art is almost hypnotic.&nbsp; He draws SM in such poses as to suggest a spider, and he does it so well that I find myself counting Spider-Man's limbs to assure myself that he has no more than four.\"",
  "decoder_ring": "I think it's important to make a callout to the second Tobey Maguire <i>Spider-Man 2</i> movie from 2004 that introduced Doctor Octopus to the big screen.&nbsp; I consider this the finest superhero movie ever made, and while a large part of what makes the movie work so well is the character work done with Peter Parker, it never forgets that the villain too is a human being, and I especially like the line near the end of the movie [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"I will not die a monster!\"]\n\nAnd while I'm on that topic, the train scene is my favorite sequence that shows vintage Spider-Man in action.&nbsp; While the plane rescue scene from 2006's <i>Superman Returns</i> is certainly the most impressive save in all of comic book movie history (and a scene I never tire of watching), the best overall superhero <i>movie</i> is, without question, <i>Spider-Man 2</i>, and the train sequence does an excellent job showing him pulling off a feat that is seemingly <i>beyond</i> his capabilities (which was exactly the <i>point</i>).",
  "highlight": "Doctor Octopus makes his entrance, and Spider-Man learns the cost of confidence."
}